# Interactive build architecture selector

Implement `tools/build/adapter-inbound/arch.py` as a minimal checklist window
that lets the
user select one or more supported build targets and saves the decision to
`.cache/build/data/arch.json`.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Offer Android ARM64, iOS ARM64, Linux ARM64, Linux AMD64, macOS ARM64,
  Windows ARM64, and Windows AMD64 as the canonical initial target set.
- [x] Present AMD64 targets with a user-facing label such as
  `AMD64 / x86-64 (64-bit PC)` so users do not need to know the architecture
  alias; keep the canonical machine-readable architecture value as `amd64`.
- [x] Detect the current host operating system and architecture and annotate the
  exact matching selectable target with a clear marker such as
  `← this is your system`; do not silently select or exclude other targets.
- [x] Do not offer tvOS or macOS Intel / x86-64 as planned canonical targets;
  keep macOS ARM64 in the supported target set.
- [x] Revisit tvOS or macOS Intel / x86-64 only through an explicit issue or
  pull request with substantial user demand and contributors able to test the
  target; neither target may block the canonical build flow.
- [x] Require at least one selected target, permit selecting every supported
  target, and expose one minimal Save action.
- [x] Write a versioned `.cache/build/data/arch.json`; manual edits are
  unsupported but must remain schema-validatable by the build runner.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency is required beyond the declared repository and
platform prerequisites.

## Evidence

Implemented by `tools/build/adapter-inbound/arch.py`. The selector exposes
exactly seven
canonical targets, keeps every checkbox initially clear, annotates the exact
Windows AMD64 host as `← this is your system`, rejects tvOS and unknown target
identifiers, and atomically writes `shar.build.arch.v1` decision JSON. CLI
validation exercised multi-target persistence and rejection paths without
opening the interactive window.
